                               </property>
                               <property name="toolTip">
                                <string>&lt;b&gt;SBAS messages file&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Selects the SBAS processing mode (1F -single frequency SBAS- and SBAS DFMC. Supported file formats are RINEX-B 2.11, EMS 2.0 or Multi-Band EMS.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;SBAS files can be found in the links provided in the following menu:&lt;br&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#0000FF&quot;&gt;Help-&gt;Download data files-&gt;SBAS Files&lt;/font&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;SBAS message files (any file format) may contain any combination of data:&lt;ul&gt;&lt;li&gt;Data from multiple GEOs.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;Data from SBAS 1F and SBAS DFMC.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;Any combination of multiple GEOs and SBAS data messages (1F and DFMC).&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;&lt;b&gt;NOTES:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;ul&gt;&lt;li&gt;If data for both SBAS 1F and SBAS DFMC is provided, only the data according to the SBAS processing mode (1F or DFMC) will be used.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;SBAS DFMC messages are also supported in EMS 2.0 files (with the same format as SBAS 1F messages). gLAB will be able to detect to which type (SBAS 1F or SBAS DFMC) each message corresponds by checking the preamble of the message.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;To process multiple days in a single run, SBAS data files from multiple days must be concatenated (in chronological order) with or without header (recommended with header). To concatenate files, use the following command line instruction:&lt;ul&gt;&lt;li&gt;&lt;b&gt;Linux/Mac/Cygwin terminal:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;cat file1 file2 file3 ... file N &gt; &lt;i&gt;Concatenated_file&lt;/i&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;&lt;b&gt;Windows terminal:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;copy /b file1+file2+...+fileN &lt;i&gt;Concatenated_file&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;&lt;br&gt;File1, file2, etc, are the input files to be concatenated. &lt;i&gt;Concatenated_file&lt;/i&gt; is the resulting file after concatenating all input files into one.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Note that gLAB does not care about filename or extensions (as gLAB will check that the file contents matches the expected one), so any filename or extension can be used for the concatenated file.&lt;br&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;</string>
                               </property>
                               <property name="whatsThis">
                                <string/>
                               </property>
                               <item>
                                <property name="text">
                                 <string>SBAS 1F File</string>
                                </property>
                               </item>
                               <item>
                                <property name="text">
                                 <string>SBAS DFMC File</string>
                                </property>
                               </item>
                              </widget>
                             </item>
