                        <item row="0" column="0" colspan="4">
                         <widget class="QLabel" name="label_282">
                          <property name="styleSheet">
                           <string notr="true">color: rgb(0, 0, 0);</string>
                          </property>
                          <property name="text">
                           <string>If  sample at 0h 0m is present in both days,
select which one to use:</string>
                          </property>
                         </widget>
                        </item>
                        <item row="1" column="1" colspan="2" alignment="Qt::AlignLeft">
                         <widget class="QFrame" name="frame_34">
                          <property name="minimumSize">
                           <size>
                            <width>191</width>
                            <height>0</height>
                           </size>
                          </property>
                          <property name="frameShape">
                           <enum>QFrame::NoFrame</enum>
                          </property>
                          <property name="frameShadow">
                           <enum>QFrame::Raised</enum>
                          </property>
                          <layout class="QGridLayout" name="gridLayout_374">
                           <property name="leftMargin">
                            <number>0</number>
                           </property>
                           <property name="topMargin">
                            <number>0</number>
                           </property>
                           <property name="rightMargin">
                            <number>0</number>
                           </property>
                           <property name="bottomMargin">
                            <number>0</number>
                           </property>
                           <item row="0" column="0">
                            <widget class="QRadioButton" name="radioButtonOrbitCurrentDayCompare">
                             <property name="maximumSize">
                              <size>
                               <width>96</width>
                               <height>16777215</height>
                              </size>
                             </property>
                             <property name="toolTip">
                              <string>&lt;b&gt;Current Day&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;This value is only valid for orbits obtained from &lt;b&gt;SP3&lt;/b&gt; files, as they require an interpolation.&lt;br&gt;When interpolating orbits from precise products, when using single day orbit files, at the beginning and at the end of the day, the interpolated value will have an additional error of few centimetres due to a non homogenous distribution (in time) of the samples used for interpolation (Runge effect). To avoid this effect, the user can introduce a concatenated orbit file from several days (ideally, from the previous and the following day), so gLAB can interpolate using always samples with homogenous distribution (when a concatenated file is introduced, gLAB will read the file using a sliding data window of three days).&lt;br&gt;In some orbit files (typically in MGEX files), the last sample given from one day is the sample at 0 hours and 0 minutes from the following day (i.e. the first sample from the next day). The problem is that this last sample has a similar (but not equal) value to the first sample of the next day (after concatenating an orbit files, this samples appears twice), therefore, depending on the sample used, the interpolation value will slightly vary during several hours (as orbit use a high interpolation degree, and date rate is typically of 15 minutes).&lt;br&gt;If this option is set, the first sample read (the one at the end of the current day), will be used for interpolation.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;NOTE:&lt;/b&gt; To see the Runge effect in the interpolation, the user can do a comparation of the same SP3 file, but using one concatenated and another without concatenation in the 'Compare Orbits &amp;amp; Clocks' mode of gLAB (the current mode).&lt;br&gt;</string>
                             </property>
                             <property name="styleSheet">
                              <string notr="true">color: rgb(0, 0, 0);</string>
                             </property>
                             <property name="text">
                              <string>Current Day</string>
                             </property>
                            </widget>
                           </item>
                           <item row="0" column="1">
                            <widget class="QRadioButton" name="radioButtonOrbitNextDayCompare">
                             <property name="maximumSize">
                              <size>
                               <width>92</width>
                               <height>16777215</height>
                              </size>
                             </property>
                             <property name="toolTip">
                              <string>&lt;b&gt;Next Day&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;This value is only valid for orbits obtained from &lt;b&gt;SP3&lt;/b&gt; files, as they require an interpolation.&lt;br&gt;When interpolating orbits from precise products, when using single day orbit files, at the beginning and at the end of the day, the interpolated value will have an additional error of few centimetres due to a non homogenous distribution (in time) of the samples used for interpolation (Runge effect). To avoid this effect, the user can introduce a concatenated orbit file from several days (ideally, from the previous and the following day), so gLAB can interpolate using always samples with homogenous distribution (when a concatenated file is introduced, gLAB will read the file using a sliding data window of three days).&lt;br&gt;In some orbit files (typically in MGEX files), the last sample given from one day is the sample at 0 hours and 0 minutes from the following day (i.e. the first sample from the next day). The problem is that this last sample has a similar (but not equal) value to the first sample of the next day (after concatenating an orbit files, this samples appears twice), therefore, depending on the sample used, the interpolation value will slightly vary during several hours (as orbit use a high interpolation degree, and date rate is typically of 15 minutes).&lt;br&gt;If this option is set, the second sample read (the one at the beginning of the next day), will be used for interpolation.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;NOTE:&lt;/b&gt; To see the Runge effect in the interpolation, the user can do a comparation of the same SP3 file, but using one concatenated and another without concatenation in the 'Compare Orbits &amp;amp; Clocks' mode of gLAB (the current mode).&lt;br&gt;</string>
                             </property>
                             <property name="styleSheet">
                              <string notr="true">color: rgb(0, 0, 0);</string>
                             </property>
                             <property name="text">
                              <string>Next Day</string>
                             </property>
                            </widget>
                           </item>
                          </layout>
                         </widget>
                        </item>
                        <item row="2" column="1">
                         <widget class="QFrame" name="frame_35">
                          <property name="minimumSize">
                           <size>
                            <width>191</width>
                            <height>0</height>
                           </size>
                          </property>
                          <property name="frameShape">
                           <enum>QFrame::NoFrame</enum>
                          </property>
                          <property name="frameShadow">
                           <enum>QFrame::Raised</enum>
                          </property>
                          <layout class="QGridLayout" name="gridLayout_375">
                           <property name="leftMargin">
                            <number>0</number>
                           </property>
                           <property name="topMargin">
                            <number>0</number>
                           </property>
                           <property name="rightMargin">
                            <number>0</number>
                           </property>
                           <property name="bottomMargin">
                            <number>0</number>
                           </property>
                           <item row="0" column="0">
                            <widget class="QRadioButton" name="radioButtonClockCurrentDayCompare">
                             <property name="maximumSize">
                              <size>
                               <width>96</width>
                               <height>16777215</height>
                              </size>
                             </property>
                             <property name="toolTip">
                              <string>&lt;b&gt;Current Day&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;This value is only valid for clocks obtained from &lt;b&gt;SP3&lt;/b&gt; or &lt;b&gt;CLK&lt;/b&gt; files, as they require an interpolation.&lt;br&gt;When interpolating clocks from precise products, when using single day clock files, at the beginning and at the end of the day, the interpolated value will have an additional error of few centimetres due to a non homogenous distribution (in time) of the samples used for interpolation (Runge effect). To avoid this effect, the user can introduce a concatenated clock file from several days (ideally, from the previous and the following day), so gLAB can interpolate using always samples with homogenous distribution (when a concatenated file is introduced, gLAB will read the file using a sliding data window of three days).&lt;br&gt;In some clock files (typically in MGEX files), the last sample given from one day is the sample at 0 hours and 0 minutes from the following day (i.e. the first sample from the next day). The problem is that this last sample has a similar (but not equal) value to the first sample of the next day (after concatenating an orbit files, this samples appears twice), therefore, depending on the sample used, the interpolation value will slightly vary during some seconds or minutes (depending on the clock data rate).&lt;br&gt;If this option is set, the first sample read (the one at the end of the current day), will be used for interpolation.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;NOTE:&lt;/b&gt; To see the Runge effect in the interpolation, the user can do a comparation of the same SP3 file, but using one concatenated and another without concatenation in the 'Compare 'Compare Orbits &amp;amp; Clocks' mode of gLAB (the current mode).&lt;br&gt;</string>
                             </property>
                             <property name="styleSheet">
                              <string notr="true">color: rgb(0, 0, 0);</string>
                             </property>
                             <property name="text">
                              <string>Current Day</string>
                             </property>
                             <property name="autoExclusive">
                              <bool>true</bool>
                             </property>
                            </widget>
                           </item>
                           <item row="0" column="1" alignment="Qt::AlignLeft">
                            <widget class="QRadioButton" name="radioButtonClockNextDayCompare">
                             <property name="maximumSize">
                              <size>
                               <width>92</width>
                               <height>16777215</height>
                              </size>
                             </property>
                             <property name="toolTip">
                              <string>&lt;b&gt;Next Day&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;This value is only valid for clocks obtained from &lt;b&gt;SP3&lt;/b&gt; or &lt;b&gt;CLK&lt;/b&gt; files, as they require an interpolation.&lt;br&gt;When interpolating clocks from precise products, when using single day clock files, at the beginning and at the end of the day, the interpolated value will have an additional error of few centimetres due to a non homogenous distribution (in time) of the samples used for interpolation (Runge effect). To avoid this effect, the user can introduce a concatenated clock file from several days (ideally, from the previous and the following day), so gLAB can interpolate using always samples with homogenous distribution (when a concatenated file is introduced, gLAB will read the file using a sliding data window of three days).&lt;br&gt;In some clock files (typically in MGEX files), the last sample given from one day is the sample at 0 hours and 0 minutes from the following day (i.e. the first sample from the next day). The problem is that this last sample has a similar (but not equal) value to the first sample of the next day (after concatenating an orbit files, this samples appears twice), therefore, depending on the sample used, the interpolation value will slightly vary during some seconds or minutes (depending on the clock data rate).&lt;br&gt;If this option is set, the second sample read (the one at the beginning of the next day), will be used for interpolation.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;NOTE:&lt;/b&gt; To see the Runge effect in the interpolation, the user can do a comparation of the same SP3 file, but using one concatenated and another without concatenation in the 'Compare Orbits &amp;amp; Clocks' mode of gLAB (the current mode).&lt;br&gt;</string>
                             </property>
                             <property name="styleSheet">
                              <string notr="true">color: rgb(0, 0, 0);</string>
                             </property>
                             <property name="text">
                              <string>Next Day</string>
                             </property>
                             <property name="autoExclusive">
                              <bool>true</bool>
                             </property>
                            </widget>
                           </item>
                          </layout>
                         </widget>
                        </item>
                        <item row="1" column="3">
                         <spacer name="horizontalSpacer_56">
                          <property name="orientation">
                           <enum>Qt::Horizontal</enum>
                          </property>
